Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-05-02
Subject: Use first char address for ptconst byte buffer
License: expat

`compiler/ptconst.pas` uses a local `array[0..1] of char` as scratch
storage for a one-character typed constant, then reads that scratch storage
through a `pbyte` cursor.

The original `ca := @ch` relies on assigning the address of the whole array
to a byte pointer.  Point at the first character explicitly instead; this
keeps the same byte-buffer semantics while avoiding a whole-array pointer
conversion in the translated bootstrap.

--- a/compiler/ptconst.pas
+++ b/compiler/ptconst.pas
@@ -916,7 +916,7 @@ implementation
                       else
                         internalerror(2010033002);
                     end;
-                    ca:=@ch;
+                    ca:=pbyte(@ch[0]);
                     len:=1;
                   end
                else
